Below is a small, self‑contained C# console application that demonstrates a variety of property patterns you’ll see in real code:

```csharp
using System;

namespace PropertyDemo
{
    // 1️⃣  A classic POCO with backing fields and validation
    public class Person
    {
        // Backing field for the FirstName property
        private string _firstName;

        // Auto‑implemented property – no custom logic
        public string LastName { get; set; }

        // ① Read‑only property that uses an expression‑bodied member
        public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";

        // ② Property with a backing field that includes validation
        public string FirstName
        {
            get => _firstName;
            set
            {
                if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value))
                    throw new ArgumentException("First name cannot be empty.");
                _firstName = value;
            }
        }

        // ③ Property with a private setter – read‑only from outside
        public int Age { get; private set; }

        // ④ Constructor that sets the read‑only Age property
        public Person(string firstName, string lastName, int age)
        {
            FirstName = firstName;      // triggers validation
            LastName  = lastName;
            Age       = age;            // private set, so can be set only here
        }

        // ⑤ Method to "age" the person
        public void HaveBirthday() => Age++;
    }

    // 6️⃣  A simple struct that uses an indexer – a different kind of property
    public struct Vector3
    {
        private readonly float[] _components = new float[3];

        public float X
        {
            get => _components[0];
            set => _components[0] = value;
        }

        public float Y
        {
            get => _components[1];
            set => _components[1] = value;
        }

        public float Z
        {
            get => _components[2];
            set => _components[2] = value;
        }

        // Indexer that lets you access components by index
        public float this[int index]
        {
            get => _components[index];
            set => _components[index] = value;
        }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // 7️⃣  Using the Person class
            var p = new Person("Ada", "Lovelace", 30);

            Console.WriteLine($"Full name: {p.FullName}");     // Ada Lovelace
            Console.WriteLine($"Age: {p.Age}");                // 30

            // The Age property has a private setter – we can only modify it inside Person
            p.HaveBirthday();
            Console.WriteLine($"After birthday: {p.Age}");     // 31

            // 8️⃣  Demonstrating the backing‑field validation
            try
            {
                p.FirstName = "";    // throws
            }
            catch (ArgumentException ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Validation error: {ex.Message}");
            }

            // 9️⃣  Using the Vector3 struct and its indexer
            var v = new Vector3 { X = 1.0f, Y = 2.0f, Z = 3.0f };
            Console.WriteLine($"Vector: ({v[0]}, {v[1]}, {v[2]})");
        }
    }
}
```

### What you’ll notice

| Feature | Code snippet | What it demonstrates |
|---------|--------------|----------------------|
| **Auto‑implemented property** | `public string LastName { get; set; }` | Simple, no backing field required. |
| **Expression‑bodied read‑only property** | `public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";` | Compact syntax for read‑only computed values. |
| **Backing field with validation** | `FirstName` property | Shows how to enforce rules when setting a value. |
| **Private setter** | `public int Age { get; private set; }` | Read‑only from the caller, only modifiable inside the class. |
| **Constructor setting read‑only property** | `Age = age;` in constructor | Allows initialization of a property with a private setter. |
| **Method that mutates a property** | `public void HaveBirthday() => Age++;` | Demonstrates changing a property from within the class. |
| **Indexers** | `public float this[int index]` | A property that can be accessed like an array. |
| **Struct with properties** | `Vector3` | Shows that structs can have properties and indexers just like classes. |

Run the program and you’ll see all the console output that confirms each concept in action. This should give you a solid, practical reference for how properties are used in everyday C# code.
